Do not reuse a literal one word beyond the reach of ldr.

With ARM VFP enabled, find_literal() reused a written literal as long as it
was at most 4088 bytes behind the load. The load is relative to pc + 8, so a
literal exactly 4088 bytes back needs the displacement -4096, which does not
fit the 12-bit immediate. The encoded instruction lost the offset and set the
low bit of the destination register: it loaded the next instruction word, or
loaded into the neighbouring register. Require the literal to be closer, as
the variant without VFP already does.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/cpu/arm/BinaryAssembler_arm.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/cpu/arm/BinaryAssembler_arm.cpp
@@ -288,11 +288,14 @@

   LiteralPoolElement* literal = _first_literal;

-  // Search for a bound literal not too far from current instruction
+  // Search for a bound literal not too far from current instruction.
+  // The load is relative to pc + 8: a literal at min_offset would need the
+  // displacement -(offset + 8), the whole range given by the caller, which
+  // the 12-bit immediate of ldr cannot encode.
   {
     const int min_offset = _code_offset - offset;
     for( ; literal && literal->is_bound(); literal = literal->next() ) {
-      if( literal->_bci >= min_offset && literal->matches(obj, imm32)) {
+      if( literal->_bci > min_offset && literal->matches(obj, imm32)) {
         return literal;
       }
     }
